WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.118 --> 00:00:05.970
- Hi, everyone.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:07.890
I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.450
the Director of the Yale Program

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.520
for the Study of Anti-Semitism.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.580
And I wanna welcome you
to this special webinar

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.810
entitled, "Anti-Semitism After October 7".

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.210
I know that while the
war is still going on,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:25.710
it's hard to think about anything,
except anxiety and grief.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.680
But we nevertheless need
to begin to take stock

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.380
of the way that the
massacre of October 7th

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:35.280
has profoundly changed the
way Jews view the world

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.400
and the way the world views Jews.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:41.430
To help us sort through
this complex situation,

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:45.150
I've invited Kenneth Stern
to speak with us today.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.010
He's an author and attorney,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.690
and serves as the Director

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.690
of the Bard Center for the Study of Hate.

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.340
Previously, he was Director

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.880
of the Division on
Anti-Semitism and Extremism

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:58.770
at the American Jewish Committee,

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.380
where he worked for 25 years.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:04.500
Mr. Stern's op-eds and book reviews

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.907
have appeared in "The New York Times",

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:09.570
"The Washington Post", "The Forward",

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:13.890
and the "Jewish Telegraphic
Agency", and other places too.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:17.190
His newest book, the
"Conflict Over the Conflict:

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:22.050
the Israel/Palestine Campus
Debate" was published in 2020.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.020
I'm very grateful to Ken
for agreeing to join us

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:27.330
on very little notice today.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.180
I can think of few people better qualified

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.123
to discuss the issue of anti-Semitism.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.440
So here's how the webinar will work.

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:42.440
I will engage Ken in discussion
for about 30 minutes.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:45.660
Then we'll take 30 minutes of
questions from the audience.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.140
And we will end right around 6:00 PM.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:52.410
So please submit questions at any time

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:55.740
during the discussion
using the Q&amp;A function

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:57.660
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.130
And I will relay the questions
during the question period.

00:02:02.130 --> 00:02:05.070
The chat function has been disabled.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:08.730
And I should say that American
Sign Language interpretation

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:12.120
is being provided live and can be accessed

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.810
by pinning the ASL interpreters.

00:02:15.810 --> 00:02:19.440
So I realize this topic has
aroused a lot of passion,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.380
but I want to discuss it today in as calm

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.750
and rational a manner as possible.

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:28.500
And I urge you to write
questions in that spirit.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:31.620
And I urge you to keep
the questions focused

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.480
on the issue of anti-Semitism.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:35.610
You know, there are many other topics,

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:38.280
like the how Israel
should conduct the war,

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.030
things like that that we
really can't get to today.

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:45.630
So with that said, let's
start on the discussion.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:47.613
There's a lot to get to.

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.067
So hi, Ken.
- Maurice, thank you.

00:02:51.067 --> 00:02:52.800
- Thank you, thank you for inviting me,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.500
and thank you for giving the opportunity

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:56.547
to talk with your audience.

00:02:56.547 --> 00:02:58.800
As you know, I'm a fan of your work.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.630
And I'm just honored to be able

00:03:00.630 --> 00:03:02.550
to have this discussion with you tonight.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.750
- Oh, well, thank you so much.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.810
And welcome to everybody here.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:07.740
So let's get started.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.620
So right after October 7th,

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.800
as the horror of the massacre emerged,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.990
there was an outpouring of revulsion

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:19.080
against Hamas and sympathy for Israel.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.150
Then things began to change

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:24.870
when Israel's bombing of Gaza started.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:28.200
Since then, we've seen some
really scary Anti-Semitic

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.090
incidents around the world,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:32.370
including just in the
last few days, things...

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:35.490
It seems like things have been
getting dramatically worse.

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.220
The attack on the airport in Dagestan

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:40.080
was particularly scary.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:43.050
And in the US, things
have gotten very scary.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:46.983
The Anti-Defamation League
last week reported a 388%

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:52.020
increase in anti-Semitic
attack since October 7th,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.210
compared with a year earlier.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:59.210
So can you start by just saying
what you think is going on?

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:02.580
How worried should we be?

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:05.400
Is this a blip or a sea change?

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.320
And maybe another way of saying that is,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.140
don't we always see a spike
in violence against Jews

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.730
after conflicts of the Middle East,

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.320
and then things settle down?

00:04:13.320 --> 00:04:14.160
But maybe not.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.710
I don't know, how do you see things?

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.350
- Well, it's very hard
to predict the future.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.110
So we're at a moment now in the middle

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:25.470
of things being totally shaken up.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.843
But you're right, if
you look historically,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:33.060
there was a concern in
the '73 Yum Kippur War

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:35.460
where there was the Arab oil embargo.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:37.260
And those of us who were
old enough to remember,

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.270
you know, we had to go
on even days and odd days

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:40.380
to fill up our tanks.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.660
And it was a concern that
Jews were gonna be blamed,

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.043
and they really weren't.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:50.820
There was the attacks that happened really

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.137
after the beginning of the second

00:04:52.137 --> 00:04:53.250
(audio skips)

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:56.370
after the peace process
collapsed, and there was a spike.

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.340
To me, it was very reminiscent
of what we're seeing,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.280
maybe on a somewhat smaller scale.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:03.480
But it was...

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.910
I remember the places in the United States

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.550
and North America having some synagogues

00:05:08.550 --> 00:05:11.550
that were being attacked,
much more so in Europe.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:13.620
And then, what happened was in the US

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.350
after a few weeks after
the shooting stopped,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:20.430
it dissipated not so much
in Europe a little bit,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:23.553
but it still was at a level
a lot higher than before.

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:28.530
What may be different now
is that we're at a point

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:33.530
where the end of what's
happening is not clear.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:40.340
And so, the continuing seeing
of pictures of civilians,

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:45.450
and Gaza being pulled outta rubble,

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.820
and babies in hospitals, and so forth,

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.000
I think are gonna contribute to some

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:54.423
of the continued attention to this.

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:00.470
And on campuses where
we've seen issues before,

00:06:02.580 --> 00:06:05.580
the couple of the pro...

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:08.360
I think it was 2000 and...

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:11.533
It's either 2000...

00:06:11.533 --> 00:06:13.140
I have the note down here, 2014.

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:16.290
Yeah, the attack, the war with Gaza,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:17.460
Israel was in July.

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:18.900
Campus was out.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:22.020
And in 2008, it was
January, so campus was out.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:25.140
So that's one of the other
different factors here

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.750
now is students are very much on campus

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.790
and organizing around this.

00:06:29.790 --> 00:06:31.560
- So on that question,

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:33.780
I know a lot of people are just shocked

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:36.543
by what's going on on college campuses.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:38.850
How do you see it?

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.600
Are campuses hotbeds of anti-Semitism?

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.240
Is it only at certain campuses?

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:47.160
It seems like the Ivy League campuses

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.410
are getting the most attention, but...

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.690
And certainly what happened at Cornell

00:06:51.690 --> 00:06:54.990
this week with those horrifying threats,

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:57.630
you know, from a another student.

00:06:57.630 --> 00:06:59.310
So how do you understand what's happening

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.603
on college campuses?

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:02.520
- Well, you're right.

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:04.380
I mean, some of the incidents we've seen

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.940
have been particularly disgusting,

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:07.860
the Cornell one in particular.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.690
But there have been others too,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.673
threats of violence and so forth.

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:16.380
But in some ways, this isn't new.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:18.180
I mean, you mentioned that I wrote a book

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:22.320
on the conflict over the conflict in 2020.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.600
And there was already, again,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.910
not on every college of 4,000 colleges.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:30.120
Israel is not a big issue on most of them.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:31.470
But on some of them, it is.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.540
And on those campuses,
what's been transpiring

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:41.540
for a number of years is
this sort of binary view,

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.847
where people get into
their buckets of thinking,

00:07:45.847 --> 00:07:49.380
"Ah, this only justice
on the Palestinian side.

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:52.893
There's no justice on the
Israeli side," or vice versa,

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.260
and trying to suppress
speech on each side.

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.170
So we've had over the years,

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:05.160
although not as ubiquitous
as some people would portray,

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.670
incidents where Israeli
or or pro-Israel speakers

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:10.500
were shouted down, a heckler's veto.

00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:14.164
We've had attempts to...

00:08:14.164 --> 00:08:15.870
You know, professors who have decided

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.210
that their politics are more important

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:21.270
to them than the responsibility
to their students,

00:08:21.270 --> 00:08:23.220
so I'm not gonna write a
letter of recommendation

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:25.680
for somebody to go study in Israel.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.010
You've had a push from time to time

00:08:29.010 --> 00:08:32.010
to have academic boycotts of Israel

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:36.960
because people were so
upset with the variety

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:39.810
of things that Israel
stands for or has done.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:43.710
Not recognizing that
in an academic setting,

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:45.480
the purpose of it is to look at ideas.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.020
And when you segregate a
nationality, that's an abrogation.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:52.140
So we've had that on one side.

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.620
We've also had actually last
year more of an escalation too,

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:59.085
where you've had at Berkeley, Vermont,

00:08:59.085 --> 00:09:03.900
and New Paltz some decisions
to exclude Zionists

00:09:03.900 --> 00:09:06.840
from spaces, like climate change,

00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:09.570
or sexual survivor spaces,

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:13.950
which tells me that it's
sort of McCarthyism.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:17.220
It's they have a right to
define their political groups.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.300
But when you say a Zionist
shouldn't be in this space,

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.700
it's the same as saying
the '50s Communists

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:24.533
can't be in the space,

00:09:24.533 --> 00:09:28.440
even though it has nothing to
do with this, the politics.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:31.410
On the flip side of this binary,

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:33.870
which I think is very
caustic to the campus,

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:38.380
you've had attempts by the
organized Jewish community

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.813
to suppress some pro-Palestinian speech.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.000
And there's a whole long
history of trying to use the,

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:51.180
what's called the IHRA
definition of anti-Semitism,

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:52.180
which I was the lead drafter

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:55.680
of in its early text and
its earlier iteration,

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:57.540
and trying to use it as a speech code

00:09:57.540 --> 00:09:59.310
to stop certain speech on campus.

00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:00.350
And we just had...

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:03.720
I think most people on
this call would find,

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.930
the Students for Justice in Palestine

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:08.430
statements over the last couple weeks

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:10.410
were reprehensible or worse.

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:13.830
But you had Jonathan Greenblatt going

00:10:13.830 --> 00:10:17.340
and Ken Marcus telling universities

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:20.640
to go investigate your DeSantis
kicking off SJP groups.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.590
That's also a violation
of academic freedom.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:26.670
So you've had this sort
of divide beforehand,

00:10:26.670 --> 00:10:30.633
but it's become on
steroids now on the campus.

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:36.830
- And that certainly was
the case with the reaction

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:40.530
to the Harvard students
who came out right,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:45.530
pretty soon after October
7th with a statement

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.250
blaming Israel for the attacks.

00:10:47.250 --> 00:10:50.460
And then, there was really
a backlash against them

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.220
and attempts to make
their identities known,

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.680
so people don't hire them.

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.760
What would you...

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.830
Do you think that was an overreaction

00:10:58.830 --> 00:11:02.700
on the part of the Jewish community there,

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.000
or Jewish donors to the Harvard students?

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.000
And if so, what would've
been a better response?

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:12.960
- Yeah, I think it was pretty awful.

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:16.083
I mean, these are 18, 19 and 20-year-olds.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:19.230
And you're going after them

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:20.940
and saying they shouldn't be employed.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:23.430
They should have a mark against 'em.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:24.263
It's a blacklist.

00:11:24.263 --> 00:11:26.790
I mean, the reason why I'm against

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:30.660
the academic boycott is also
because it's a blacklist.

00:11:30.660 --> 00:11:34.140
And I don't think I
blacklist with blacklists,

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:37.710
the doxxing and the
inherent threat of violence

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:39.210
that comes with we know where you live

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:42.270
and we're gonna put a truck
in front of your house,

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:45.450
you know, to me is disgusting,
but that's not new.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.670
I mean, there's a thing
called Canary Mission,

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:52.450
which has been around and
has identified students

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:56.760
that the Canary Mission
folks have objection

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:58.290
to some of their speech.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:00.390
And I share a lot of their objections.

00:12:00.390 --> 00:12:03.123
But when you create a list
that sort of marks them,

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.090
that's something-
- So what's a better...

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:08.040
- What should we do then?

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:09.030
Yeah.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:11.670
- Yeah, so I think you
speak out against it.

00:12:11.670 --> 00:12:13.020
I think you encourage others to

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:16.170
speak out against that type of speech.

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:21.170
You don't try to penalize it
because it is free speech.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:24.930
But you use on an academy,

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:29.190
you use the tools you have at hand, right?

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:32.130
You use the educational process

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:36.720
to mine in a very difficult moment.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:39.090
And there are things that
you can do short-term

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.703
and things that you can do long-term.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:46.710
Short-term, I know a colleague of of ours,

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.360
Susanna Heschel and a few others,

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:51.963
immediately did a very
good program at Dartmouth.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:55.200
There have been places,
Bard and other places too,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.330
that have done vigils.

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:59.013
I think Williams as well.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:02.550
And at Bard last Thursday,

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:05.073
I mean, Bard has had a lot of programs.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:10.970
One that we put together
was looking at the issue

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:15.300
of laws of war and
Islamophobia and anti-Semitism

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.133
in the aftermath of this.

00:13:16.133 --> 00:13:17.780
And I had a subtext for that.

00:13:17.780 --> 00:13:18.990
It was very crowded.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:22.830
It was intense discussion,
but I wanted students

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:25.620
to get out of their binary boxes

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:26.760
and not to feed the binary.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:31.760
So the binary boxes, again,
are Israel is 100% responsible.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:34.500
This is all about the occupation.

00:13:34.500 --> 00:13:37.620
It's all about settler colonialism.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:40.140
And then, the other side is the,

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:41.400
wait a minute, this is...

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.622
How could you not condemn baby
killing and some of the...

00:13:45.622 --> 00:13:48.150
I don't know if you saw
the Blinken's testimony

00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:51.180
about some of the more
gruesome things that happened,

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.550
you know, and not condemned that.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.862
So what I wanted the
students to realize was that,

00:13:56.862 --> 00:13:59.910
A, 'cause we run a center
for hate that it's not

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:01.890
the first time hate has
gotten to this point,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:04.020
where their bombs are happening,

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:07.530
and people are getting
shot, and atrocities.

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:11.850
And there's actually
a rule of how you deal

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.160
with these things, and
it's the rules of war.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.660
So I wanted them to see that

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:18.570
and where both sides may not be...

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:19.710
You know, or certainly not...

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:22.050
Well, one, either Hamas
clearly and Israel,

00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:24.180
you can point to certain questions

00:14:24.180 --> 00:14:26.670
about whether they're living
up to the laws of war.

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:31.290
And then on the anti-Semitism
and Islamophobia,

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:36.290
I wanted them to understand
precisely how hatred works.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:40.890
You know, when I have
discussions about this topic,

00:14:40.890 --> 00:14:43.833
and actually a lot of
discussions around the book,

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:47.700
I go back to the, the first chapter,

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:51.840
which is about how when our identity

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:56.430
is tethered to an issue of
social justice or injustice,

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.590
our brains actually function differently.

00:14:58.590 --> 00:15:01.141
There's brain science that show shows.

00:15:01.141 --> 00:15:02.250
That there's social psychology.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:05.820
There are other fields that show

00:15:05.820 --> 00:15:07.350
our brains fire differently,

00:15:07.350 --> 00:15:10.410
that we try to reduce
things to simplicity,

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:12.780
and all right, and all wrong.

00:15:12.780 --> 00:15:14.910
We're allergic to complexity.

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:18.000
And we just get further and
further into our own bubbles.

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:21.300
And when you sort of understand that

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:23.130
that's what happens when we feel this way,

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.260
I think it creates an intellectual space

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.140
to be able to engage with that more.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:31.110
So that was one of the things
I shared with the students.

00:15:31.110 --> 00:15:32.970
And also, about the uptick

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.433
in anti-Semitism and Islamophobia.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:39.510
I mean, there have been
the murder of that,

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:43.020
you know, six-year-old child.

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:46.440
And I have a friend in the UK

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:51.440
who monitors online
anti-Semitism and Islamophobia,

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:53.790
both, of course, have spiked tremendously.

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:55.020
But they said this is the first time

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:57.240
you've seen them happen in tandem.

00:15:57.240 --> 00:15:58.680
- Yeah.

00:15:58.680 --> 00:16:01.620
- The point I wanted
students to come away with is

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.780
this is how hatred works.

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:06.300
This animates a certain number of people

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:10.830
to feel justified to act on
it with things of violence.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:12.690
You may not act.

00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.090
You're not gonna act it in a violent way.

00:16:15.090 --> 00:16:16.980
But some of the things that stir them

00:16:16.980 --> 00:16:18.510
are probably going on with you.

00:16:18.510 --> 00:16:19.890
And I want 'em to be able to reflect.

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:20.880
So things like that.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.650
And I could talk about more
things to do on campus.

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:27.330
- Yeah, so what about just
a kind of basic vexing

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:29.670
question in anti-Semitism studies is,

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.310
how do you distinguish between legitimate

00:16:32.310 --> 00:16:36.120
criticism of Israel's
policies, even the war,

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.890
and anti-Semitism?

00:16:37.890 --> 00:16:40.740
And where do we put that line?

00:16:40.740 --> 00:16:42.330
And has...

00:16:42.330 --> 00:16:43.560
This is obviously a question I know

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:44.730
you've thought a lot about.

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.760
But has the recent war changed

00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:50.490
how you've thought about this question?

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:52.373
- It hasn't changed
how I thought about it.

00:16:52.373 --> 00:16:53.206
I think...

00:16:53.206 --> 00:16:56.580
And I wrote a piece for
an Israeli think tank

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:58.320
a couple of years ago about this.

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:02.770
That basically, anti-Semitism at its core

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:05.430
is two things, right?

00:17:05.430 --> 00:17:07.860
It's conspiracy theory that believe Jews

00:17:07.860 --> 00:17:10.470
are conspiring to harm humanity

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.410
and gives an explanation for
what goes wrong in the world,

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.980
from missing Christian children
back in the Blood Libel days

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:20.190
to Marjorie Taylor Greene and
Jewish space lasers, right?

00:17:20.190 --> 00:17:25.190
So that's the core of
how anti-Semitism works.

00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:27.810
So if one is cut and pasting Israel

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.723
and putting in that place, that's fine.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:34.830
On the other side of the spectrum,

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:37.740
there are Palestinian
folks who are saying,

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:42.740
wait a minute, Zionism means
that basically Jews came

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:49.440
and disrupted our ability
to control our lives.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:53.820
And that's why they are anti-Zionists.

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:56.010
They don't believe that there
should be a Jewish state.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.490
Do they believe that Jews
should be slaughtered like this?

00:17:59.490 --> 00:18:01.770
The ones that I conversed with, no.

00:18:01.770 --> 00:18:06.770
They believe in a one state
with everybody getting a vote.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:09.540
Now, that's a problem for...

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:13.290
I mean, certainly a problem
for Jews before October 7th,

00:18:13.290 --> 00:18:15.750
when who's gonna trust that that's

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:17.253
gonna work out hunky dory?

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:22.440
But to me, you can make the argument

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:25.440
that it's functionally
anti-Semitic and anti-Palestinian

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:29.460
because it's gonna be perpetual warfare.

00:18:29.460 --> 00:18:32.220
But does that mean that they're
motivated by hatred of Jews?

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:33.053
No.

00:18:34.410 --> 00:18:35.640
And when I teach about this,

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:39.360
I use a essay by Derek Penslar of Harvard.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.730
It's sort of a what if essay.

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:45.210
And he talks about, what if it had been,

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:48.660
instead of Jews coming back
to their historic homeland,

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.930
it had been a Protestant
group called the Templars

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:53.760
that had come and reconstituted?

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.973
There wouldn't have been more
like the reaction to Rhodesia.

00:18:58.170 --> 00:19:02.010
So to me, there's clear instances

00:19:02.010 --> 00:19:05.430
where anti-Zionism is anti-Semitism,

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:10.430
clear instances where
anti-Zionism is not anti-Semitism.

00:19:10.770 --> 00:19:12.750
And there's a whole bunch
of gray in the middle,

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:17.040
depending on intent, and
context, and so forth.

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.020
- Yeah, so one of those instances...

00:19:19.020 --> 00:19:21.510
So last week on the Yale campus,

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:25.530
there was a pro-Palestine
walkout, they called it.

00:19:25.530 --> 00:19:27.840
I saw it.

00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:30.247
And there were signs saying,

00:19:30.247 --> 00:19:33.387
"From the river to the Sea,
Palestine will be free."

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:37.230
And many, including the
Anti-Defamation League,

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:40.710
considered this slogan anti-Semitic.

00:19:40.710 --> 00:19:43.230
I think they see it as
a kind of dog whistle

00:19:43.230 --> 00:19:46.320
implying the extermination of Jews,

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:49.230
or at least the erasure of Israel.

00:19:49.230 --> 00:19:51.540
But other people don't
see that as anti-Semitic.

00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:55.290
So that seems to be one
of the kind of gray areas.

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:59.100
How do you understand that
slogan as a concrete example?

00:19:59.100 --> 00:20:02.010
- Yeah, I mean, precisely, it's ambiguous.

00:20:02.010 --> 00:20:04.260
'Cause somebody could mean we just...

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:05.370
Yeah, from the river to the sea,

00:20:05.370 --> 00:20:07.620
we're gonna wipe out all
the Jews and take it over.

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:09.600
And that's one way that
that could be interpreted.

00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.430
It could also mean from
the river to the sea,

00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:13.140
we're gonna have a democratic society

00:20:13.140 --> 00:20:14.970
where this one person, one vote.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:19.970
And so, the context of it is important.

00:20:20.490 --> 00:20:23.610
You know, and I think one of the problems

00:20:23.610 --> 00:20:28.610
with looking about
anti-Semitism is, again,

00:20:29.310 --> 00:20:31.680
you know, we wanna get
into that comfort space

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:34.980
where we say, "Oh, it's
clearly anti-Semitic,"

00:20:34.980 --> 00:20:37.860
or, "Ah, it's not antisemitic at all."

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.410
And there are a lot of things
that are much more ambiguous.

00:20:40.410 --> 00:20:43.110
And I think to the extent that we say,

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:47.610
okay, how do we look at this problem,

00:20:47.610 --> 00:20:50.190
rather than sticking
in one pin or another,

00:20:50.190 --> 00:20:51.203
and then moving on,

00:20:51.203 --> 00:20:53.880
I think the better we
we're able to understand

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.500
and what to do about it.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:56.807
- Okay.

00:20:56.807 --> 00:20:59.100
So I'm gonna shift a little bit to talk

00:20:59.100 --> 00:21:04.100
about the American government's
reaction to October 7th.

00:21:05.820 --> 00:21:10.264
And I think it was pretty striking that

00:21:10.264 --> 00:21:12.900
the Biden administration has been,

00:21:12.900 --> 00:21:16.563
you know, very strongly,
at least initially,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:21.750
supportive of Israel in
the wake of the attack.

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:24.510
How do you understand that?

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:26.460
So this is now we're talking about maybe

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:29.700
the opposite of anti-Semitism here.

00:21:29.700 --> 00:21:30.533
Is it...

00:21:30.533 --> 00:21:34.053
And do you see it beginning to weaken now?

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:40.290
- Well, I was impressed where
the Biden administration,

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.690
some other foreign leaders too,

00:21:42.690 --> 00:21:45.090
came out because they see a lot

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:46.620
of different interests involved.

00:21:46.620 --> 00:21:49.200
One is just on the moral tone,

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:53.100
the slaughter and the brutality was,

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:55.053
you know, absolutely revolting.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:00.090
And as a leader, I presume you'd think,

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:03.207
what would you do if that
happened to your people?

00:22:03.207 --> 00:22:06.900
And what would the responsibilities be?

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.180
So the full throated support of Israel

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:13.320
to be able to deal with
this is not surprising.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.740
Now, the devil is in the details, right?

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:18.050
So there's...

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.280
You know, I'm...

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.690
Again, you don't wanna go too far

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.430
into the conflict situation,

00:22:23.430 --> 00:22:26.550
but I'm not convinced that
one can wipe out Hamas.

00:22:26.550 --> 00:22:28.050
It's an idea and maybe there'd be

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:30.150
something worse that comes.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:32.160
I'm not sure what the plans
are for the day after.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:35.910
But I've also seen the
Biden administration,

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:40.410
you know, to my satisfaction at least,

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.540
publicly talking about there...

00:22:42.540 --> 00:22:44.640
These are the lessons from Afghanistan.

00:22:44.640 --> 00:22:45.930
This is what...

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:49.590
This is the rabbit hole
you may be going down.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:52.200
You know, don't make the
same mistakes we did.

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:56.457
And then, also having
concerns for civilians.

00:22:57.450 --> 00:23:02.450
And so, whether that's gonna change,

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:04.920
the bombing continues, I don't know.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:09.920
I mean, it's certainly a
possibility that it may change.

00:23:10.410 --> 00:23:12.783
And one of the calculations, again,

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:15.480
is that you've seen some,

00:23:15.480 --> 00:23:19.830
you know, progressive
Democrats, who are...

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:22.677
You know, I fully
understand it, seeing the...

00:23:22.677 --> 00:23:24.600
You know, when you see dead babies

00:23:24.600 --> 00:23:26.750
and you don't think,
gee, this should stop.

00:23:27.810 --> 00:23:30.750
There's a challenge there.

00:23:30.750 --> 00:23:32.790
And some of those folks are saying

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:35.490
they're so upset with Biden for not

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:38.850
stopping Israel from this response,

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.070
that they're never gonna vote for him.

00:23:41.070 --> 00:23:44.880
And so, is that gonna be
something that's gonna be...

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:47.550
You know, the support of Israel
if he loses the election?

00:23:47.550 --> 00:23:49.320
Some are gonna have that narrative,

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.190
- Right, and is...

00:23:50.190 --> 00:23:52.770
You know, I mean, it
just seems to me like,

00:23:52.770 --> 00:23:56.130
okay, so that means that Trump
will be elected next time?

00:23:56.130 --> 00:23:58.500
Is that gonna be great
for progressive causes?

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:01.063
That seems to be a really, you know...

00:24:01.063 --> 00:24:03.840
I understand that perhaps they're,

00:24:03.840 --> 00:24:06.030
you know, these progressive
groups feel like

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.820
they need to threaten
loss of support for Biden.

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:10.920
But I think that that just seems

00:24:10.920 --> 00:24:13.800
like a completely irrational response.

00:24:13.800 --> 00:24:15.880
But anyway, that's my own editorializing.

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:17.430
- Yeah, yeah.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:19.860
- So let's talk a little bit more

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:24.860
about Jewish groups who are anti-Zionist.

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:27.930
Or maybe not even anti-Zionists,

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:30.690
but really oppose this war

00:24:30.690 --> 00:24:33.840
and are calling for a ceasefire.

00:24:33.840 --> 00:24:36.120
How do you understand that?

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:38.190
Is that, you know...

00:24:38.190 --> 00:24:42.060
Do you put that in the same category as,

00:24:42.060 --> 00:24:45.810
you know, different forms of anti-Zionism

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:48.300
that do cross the line to anti-Semitism?

00:24:48.300 --> 00:24:52.100
I guess another way of
saying that is, are there...

00:24:52.950 --> 00:24:54.870
You know, do you see that...

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:57.630
Are Jews exempt from anti-Semitism?

00:24:57.630 --> 00:24:58.620
Or how do you...

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:02.070
How do you understand that Jewish

00:25:02.070 --> 00:25:04.170
anti-Zionist response?

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:06.990
- Yeah, Jews aren't
exempt from anti-Semitism.

00:25:06.990 --> 00:25:08.850
Jews could be anti-Semitic, just like,

00:25:08.850 --> 00:25:11.700
you know, Black people could
be racist, and so forth.

00:25:11.700 --> 00:25:16.440
That being said, I see debate
inside the Jewish community.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:20.130
And this is one of the
things that disturbs me

00:25:20.130 --> 00:25:24.000
about some of the
pushing of the definition

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:26.880
for application on campuses saying,

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:28.890
to be Jewish means you have to have

00:25:28.890 --> 00:25:29.820
an identity with Israel.

00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:31.353
You have to be a Zionist.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:34.920
I'm a Zionist.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:38.640
Israel's a part of my Jewish identity.

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:41.250
But I realized that for some Jews,

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.810
that's not in fact the case.

00:25:42.810 --> 00:25:45.873
And I interviewed a number of them

00:25:45.873 --> 00:25:47.850
when I was putting together the book.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:51.450
And again, I don't have data
for this particular thing,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:52.590
but a lot of anecdotes.

00:25:52.590 --> 00:25:56.730
And the anecdotes sorta
go something like this.

00:25:56.730 --> 00:25:58.020
I went to Jewish day school.

00:25:58.020 --> 00:26:00.750
A lot of the people in,
if not now in particular,

00:26:00.750 --> 00:26:05.430
and also JVP, I think
some had Jewish education.

00:26:05.430 --> 00:26:08.070
And some of them are children

00:26:08.070 --> 00:26:12.840
and grandchildren of Jewish
communal staff and lay leaders.

00:26:12.840 --> 00:26:15.060
Had a Jewish education.

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:16.590
I go to college.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:17.730
And for the first time I hear

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:19.980
there's something called an occupation.

00:26:19.980 --> 00:26:22.380
And I feel betrayed in what's going on.

00:26:22.380 --> 00:26:25.110
And I learned about how
we treat the stranger.

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:27.420
I learned about repairing the world.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:30.150
I can't justify the
existence of a Jewish state

00:26:30.150 --> 00:26:32.850
on top of a Palestinian population

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:35.490
as something that
comports with my Judaism.

00:26:35.490 --> 00:26:37.020
Now, I can argue with them.

00:26:37.020 --> 00:26:38.580
I can disagree with them.

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:41.760
But who am I to say that
they're outside the tent

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:43.623
of what being Jewish should mean.

00:26:44.760 --> 00:26:45.593
- Yeah.

00:26:46.800 --> 00:26:49.200
Okay, and maybe one last
question that I'll ask you

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:53.580
before going to the
questions from the audience.

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:58.580
And I do urge you all to put
questions in the Q&amp;A function.

00:27:00.270 --> 00:27:05.270
So who has a right to
talk about these issues?

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:10.730
You know, I think that
a lot of Jews feel like,

00:27:13.110 --> 00:27:16.456
you know, are suspicious when...

00:27:16.456 --> 00:27:19.080
You know, they can certainly
understand why Palestinians,

00:27:19.080 --> 00:27:22.290
maybe all Arabs, are concerned about this.

00:27:22.290 --> 00:27:24.720
They can maybe understand why other Jews

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:27.030
are concerned about this on both sides.

00:27:27.030 --> 00:27:29.070
But I think there's
sometimes some suspicion

00:27:29.070 --> 00:27:33.330
why non-Jews are so energized
about this question.

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:35.370
And how do you see that?

00:27:35.370 --> 00:27:38.850
Like, who has a right to
talk about these issues

00:27:38.850 --> 00:27:42.480
on campuses or elsewhere?

00:27:42.480 --> 00:27:44.910
- I think anybody has a right
to talk about these issues.

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:47.310
But the sort of...

00:27:47.310 --> 00:27:51.093
If you look back at the
sort of timeline of where,

00:27:52.230 --> 00:27:55.920
you know, there's sort of
been a left-wing critique

00:27:55.920 --> 00:27:59.040
of Israel, it's an interesting timeline.

00:27:59.040 --> 00:28:02.190
And it goes a lot with the politics

00:28:02.190 --> 00:28:03.690
and progressive movement,

00:28:03.690 --> 00:28:06.033
which I consider myself
part of, historically.

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:07.950
If you look back at

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:12.087
the National Lawyer's
Guild Resolution of 1948...

00:28:12.087 --> 00:28:16.263
And the Lawyer's Guild is a
progressive organization that

00:28:16.263 --> 00:28:20.310
has been very much in
the forefront of work,

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:23.283
opposing the Israeli government.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:28.500
1948 resolution reads
like a Leon Uris novel.

00:28:28.500 --> 00:28:30.510
It's like you support the Haganah.

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:32.580
The Arabs are attacking, why?

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:34.743
Because at that moment in time,

00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.420
Israel was seen as socialist
and an anti-colonialist.

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.550
And the Russians were supportive.

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:44.403
And so, that was the political dynamic.

00:28:45.480 --> 00:28:50.340
Then you have, I think
'67 was a watershed,

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:55.340
where a lot of progressives,
and non-aligned countries,

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:58.860
and so forth said, "What's going on here?"

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.320
And started to see this is a flip to

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.460
adopting the Palestinian narrative,

00:29:05.460 --> 00:29:08.163
as opposed to the Israeli one.

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:13.710
Then you started seeing the left.

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:15.600
I mean, if you look at
the political statement

00:29:15.600 --> 00:29:16.560
of the Weather Underground,

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:19.203
it reads like contemporary thing from SJP.

00:29:21.090 --> 00:29:22.470
So it's been part of the...

00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:24.000
I think, there were times where there

00:29:24.000 --> 00:29:26.580
were certain movements that
people coalesced around,

00:29:26.580 --> 00:29:30.480
South Africa, and anti-war, and so forth.

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:32.070
And this has the imagination,

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:34.670
partly because I think a
lot of people are concerned

00:29:35.520 --> 00:29:39.060
because the Holy Land
is an important place.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:40.530
It's a US ally.

00:29:40.530 --> 00:29:43.890
It gets a lot of of money,
gets a lot of attention.

00:29:43.890 --> 00:29:48.870
But it's also become one of those binary,

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:52.650
you know, good and bad symbols.

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:56.520
And again, when we're
talking about a campus,

00:29:56.520 --> 00:30:00.090
the thing to me is to try
to break down that binary

00:30:00.090 --> 00:30:02.910
to show it's more complicated.

00:30:02.910 --> 00:30:07.827
So there have been some
efforts to do that.

00:30:07.827 --> 00:30:12.317
But I'll give you one thing
that came up in my talk at Bard.

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:17.280
I mean, one of the simple or binary,

00:30:17.280 --> 00:30:18.150
how do we look at this

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:20.190
and we don't have to think about it is

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:24.460
Israel's a settler colonial
state, end of the story, okay?

00:30:24.460 --> 00:30:28.470
So it's the indigenous
people are being hurt.

00:30:28.470 --> 00:30:30.840
And I have in my book, I have
the two competing narratives.

00:30:30.840 --> 00:30:35.160
And they sort of fly
by each other at night.

00:30:35.160 --> 00:30:37.770
But I had this couplet that I pointed

00:30:37.770 --> 00:30:40.140
out to a student who's pushing this.

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:44.280
And I talked about the fact that

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:46.110
there was a group called ACBI,

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:48.510
the Palestinian Campaign for the Academic

00:30:48.510 --> 00:30:50.670
and Cultural Boycott of Israel.

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:54.993
It sent a letter to the Iroquois
Confederacy in New York,

00:30:56.370 --> 00:30:59.700
because the Iroquois folks have a...

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:00.533
Lacrosse is...

00:31:00.533 --> 00:31:02.010
They claim to have invented it.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.950
They're very much involved
in international lacrosse.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:06.990
It was gonna be an international lacrosse

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:10.260
tournament in Tel Aviv.

00:31:10.260 --> 00:31:12.150
And they said, "You're indigenous like us.

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:14.790
You're a victim of settler
colonialism like us.

00:31:14.790 --> 00:31:18.120
It'll be a betrayal for
you to come, don't."

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:21.540
And then, I contrast it with an article,

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:25.718
short couple paragraphs from Judea Pearl,

00:31:25.718 --> 00:31:28.230
you may know as, teachers at UCLA,

00:31:28.230 --> 00:31:29.850
Or (speaks indistinctly)
his son, Daniel Pearl,

00:31:29.850 --> 00:31:32.280
was the journalist who was beheaded.

00:31:32.280 --> 00:31:36.570
And he said, "Name me another
settler colonial state

00:31:36.570 --> 00:31:38.640
where you have people
speaking the language

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:40.590
that was spoken there
before the, you know,

00:31:40.590 --> 00:31:42.060
so-called indigenous folks.

00:31:42.060 --> 00:31:43.920
Name me another place
where people have been

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:46.560
talking about in their poetry, and lore,

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:49.080
and religion for thousands of years

00:31:49.080 --> 00:31:50.310
of settler colonialists.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:54.300
Name me another place
where you name cities,

00:31:54.300 --> 00:31:57.180
New York, New Berlin, New Baghdad,

00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:58.650
you know, not those things.

00:31:58.650 --> 00:32:03.210
But names that they had
in times of the Bible."

00:32:03.210 --> 00:32:07.087
And then I quote a historian who says,

00:32:07.087 --> 00:32:09.510
"Real history is the ability to navigate

00:32:09.510 --> 00:32:11.670
both those things simultaneously.

00:32:11.670 --> 00:32:13.890
The rest is communal advocacy."

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:15.600
And the more I think we can underscore

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:19.860
that complexity to look
at and really understand

00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:21.570
the narratives, that's a good thing.

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:26.570
I mean, one final point
too is I tell students,

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:29.580
and I've been speaking
on a lot of campuses

00:32:29.580 --> 00:32:31.530
even before this.

00:32:31.530 --> 00:32:32.940
You know, none of them are gonna solve

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:35.760
the Israeli-Palestinian conflict.

00:32:35.760 --> 00:32:37.260
All of them have control over

00:32:37.260 --> 00:32:39.390
how they treat their classmates.

00:32:39.390 --> 00:32:41.700
It's fine if they have strong,

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:44.223
you know, political views on this.

00:32:45.540 --> 00:32:49.860
And as part of being a good advocate,

00:32:49.860 --> 00:32:51.510
don't they want to engage with people

00:32:51.510 --> 00:32:52.650
that have a different point of view.

00:32:52.650 --> 00:32:53.940
That you'll learn more from people

00:32:53.940 --> 00:32:56.250
that have a different point of view

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:57.300
from people that agree with you.

00:32:57.300 --> 00:32:59.610
And I tell 'em, I used to
be a criminal trial lawyer.

00:32:59.610 --> 00:33:01.560
You know, if I didn't...

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:02.490
I was...

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:05.250
If I didn't think about
how the prosecution

00:33:05.250 --> 00:33:07.050
would present its case,

00:33:07.050 --> 00:33:08.940
and was just focusing
on what I wanted to say,

00:33:08.940 --> 00:33:11.070
but not having the imagination

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:12.900
to think about how they
were gonna approach it,

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:14.820
I would've been committing malpractice.

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.340
So the more you make it
as an educational good

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:19.320
to have the emotional capacity

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:21.423
and the intellectual curiosity,

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:24.270
the better off I think things will be.

00:33:24.270 --> 00:33:25.350
- Yeah, okay.

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:28.320
So we have a lot of questions.

00:33:28.320 --> 00:33:31.830
And several of the questions
are grouped around this issue.

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:35.040
So someone says, "Yesterday,
Harvard and UPenn

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:37.380
announced the formation of advisory boards

00:33:37.380 --> 00:33:39.720
to combat anti-Semitism.

00:33:39.720 --> 00:33:44.070
Why isn't the battle against
anti-Semitism included,

00:33:44.070 --> 00:33:48.390
or already included, as
part of existing DEI,"

00:33:48.390 --> 00:33:51.600
so diversity, equity and
inclusion infrastructure.

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:52.590
So as everyone...

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:54.630
Maybe people who aren't
part of universities

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:56.100
don't know that there is...

00:33:56.100 --> 00:33:58.500
You know, there is a whole DEI

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:01.200
infrastructure on every campus.

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:03.930
And Jews seem to be left out of that.

00:34:03.930 --> 00:34:04.763
So why do we...

00:34:04.763 --> 00:34:06.900
You know, why is that?

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:08.130
- It depends on where.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:12.420
There's some places where
anti-Semitism is included.

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:14.190
And I've done some
training for some places,

00:34:14.190 --> 00:34:15.247
and some places where it's not.

00:34:15.247 --> 00:34:18.540
And there are places before this,

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:20.610
that have decided they really wanted

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.040
to wrestle with issues of anti-Semitism.

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:26.760
And Lila Corwin Berman
at Temple put together

00:34:26.760 --> 00:34:30.810
a commission that really
looked at the issue.

00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:33.990
And it really just isn't a question

00:34:33.990 --> 00:34:36.810
of support for students,
which is important,

00:34:36.810 --> 00:34:38.910
or to think about it for administrators,

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:42.300
but it's also to think
about it for curriculum.

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:45.330
I think there needs to be
more classes on anti-Semitism.

00:34:45.330 --> 00:34:48.030
But there also needs
to be more classes on,

00:34:48.030 --> 00:34:49.890
how do you have difficult discussions

00:34:49.890 --> 00:34:52.050
on issues that cut you to the core,

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:54.480
about the importance of academic freedom

00:34:54.480 --> 00:34:55.680
and free speech,

00:34:55.680 --> 00:34:59.460
about I think part of
the challenge on campus,

00:34:59.460 --> 00:35:04.380
and I think this is partly
some what goes under DEI.

00:35:04.380 --> 00:35:06.570
And I'm, again, supportive of a lot of it.

00:35:06.570 --> 00:35:09.630
But I see sometimes where the message is

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:11.940
students should not be disturbed by ideas.

00:35:11.940 --> 00:35:13.077
If you're gonna be disturbed by ideas,

00:35:13.077 --> 00:35:15.240
you know, oh my God,
we've gotta protect you.

00:35:15.240 --> 00:35:16.680
You're fragile.

00:35:16.680 --> 00:35:19.650
To me, nobody should be harassed.

00:35:19.650 --> 00:35:21.390
Nobody should be discriminated against.

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:23.280
Nobody should be threatened.

00:35:23.280 --> 00:35:24.810
Nobody should be bullied.

00:35:24.810 --> 00:35:26.130
But if you're not getting disturbed

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:28.343
by ideas on a college campus, you know,

00:35:28.343 --> 00:35:30.450
why are you spending all
that money to go there?

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:32.490
- Right, but do you think that, I mean,

00:35:32.490 --> 00:35:35.760
several people have pointed out that Jews

00:35:35.760 --> 00:35:40.760
don't seem to be treated
as a minority worthy of,

00:35:42.210 --> 00:35:47.210
you know, diversity sensitivity on campus.

00:35:49.110 --> 00:35:51.933
You know, is do you feel like that's true?

00:35:52.950 --> 00:35:56.610
You know, and that
certain things that people

00:35:56.610 --> 00:35:58.140
have pointed out that are...

00:35:58.140 --> 00:35:59.820
You know, you would never hear about other

00:35:59.820 --> 00:36:01.530
minority groups are kind of tolerated

00:36:01.530 --> 00:36:04.173
when it comes to Jews on campuses?

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:08.970
- It depends on the campus
and it depends on the context.

00:36:08.970 --> 00:36:11.283
So there are a lot of campuses,

00:36:12.533 --> 00:36:15.484
(audio garbles)

00:36:15.484 --> 00:36:18.870
Jews, a lot of campuses
that have a lot of Jews.

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:23.870
There is some tendency among
some folks on the left to,

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:31.110
again, to go into these binary
spaces where Jews are white,

00:36:31.110 --> 00:36:32.820
Jews are privileged.

00:36:32.820 --> 00:36:35.847
How can you have
discrimination against them?

00:36:35.847 --> 00:36:37.320
And that's not new.

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:40.230
And that's hurtful when...

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:42.870
You know, 20, 30 years ago
when I was doing training,

00:36:42.870 --> 00:36:46.230
I was saying, for a Jewish student

00:36:46.230 --> 00:36:50.010
who hears that something
that happens to them

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:54.540
can't possibly be hurtful
because they're white,

00:36:54.540 --> 00:36:56.400
that's doubly dismissive
because they think

00:36:56.400 --> 00:37:00.810
that the people that ought
to know, understand, don't.

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:02.940
So that happens in some places.

00:37:02.940 --> 00:37:06.600
But for the majority of
what I've been able to see,

00:37:06.600 --> 00:37:09.250
again, I haven't done a
survey of all 4,000 campuses,

00:37:10.499 --> 00:37:14.790
is that if there's something
that's clearly anti-Semitic,

00:37:14.790 --> 00:37:18.663
you know, white supremacist,

00:37:19.770 --> 00:37:23.370
you know, leafleting or
something, that'll be dealt.

00:37:23.370 --> 00:37:27.886
When it comes to Israel,
it's much more complicated.

00:37:27.886 --> 00:37:31.230
And it's, again, you have Jewish students

00:37:31.230 --> 00:37:34.907
who are taking the position
that is anti-Zionist.

00:37:34.907 --> 00:37:36.270
I mean, there's a whole kerfuffle

00:37:36.270 --> 00:37:40.860
about that speech at the CUNY
graduate at the law school,

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:45.860
where somebody who's giving a
virulently anti-Israel speech.

00:37:48.060 --> 00:37:51.090
And the Jewish Law Student
Group was supportive of it.

00:37:51.090 --> 00:37:53.520
So you have a variety.

00:37:53.520 --> 00:37:55.680
And you also have Palestinian students.

00:37:55.680 --> 00:38:00.240
And when you have a pushback on something

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:03.090
that's not clearly anti-Semitic,

00:38:03.090 --> 00:38:04.470
but may make Jewish students

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:08.013
who have an Israel identity is part of it,

00:38:09.840 --> 00:38:14.840
then you do something to harm
the Palestinian students too.

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.940
So the better response is not just

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:21.330
to think about, how am I gonna
go flag that type of speech?

00:38:21.330 --> 00:38:24.390
It's, how do I bring
people together to have,

00:38:24.390 --> 00:38:25.590
you know, conversations?

00:38:25.590 --> 00:38:27.960
How should I change the academy,

00:38:27.960 --> 00:38:30.480
so that people know they're
not gonna be harassed,

00:38:30.480 --> 00:38:31.920
but they are gonna be disturbed?

00:38:31.920 --> 00:38:35.010
And then what are we gonna do
with that, being disturbed?

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:38.080
- And what about someone is asking

00:38:38.940 --> 00:38:43.940
if you really do feel that there should be

00:38:46.890 --> 00:38:50.130
an immediate ceasefire
in the war right now.

00:38:50.130 --> 00:38:52.200
And then some people are saying that

00:38:52.200 --> 00:38:55.020
that's anti-Semitic to say that.

00:38:55.020 --> 00:38:57.441
How do you respon... (laughs)

00:38:57.441 --> 00:38:59.310
How do you respond to that?

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:02.430
- God, I'm glad I don't
have to make those decisions

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:05.460
about what to do or what
not to do in this context.

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:09.543
You know, as a human
being, I'm concerned about,

00:39:12.151 --> 00:39:16.410
you know, the civilians
that are dying in Gaza.

00:39:18.000 --> 00:39:19.143
It's heartbreaking.

00:39:21.030 --> 00:39:24.810
I think, Israel has a right to defend.

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:26.310
And, well, I was asking an ethicist,

00:39:26.310 --> 00:39:29.040
who's a philosopher friend of mine.

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:30.593
You know, the beginning of this.

00:39:30.593 --> 00:39:32.370
I said, to get out the hostages,

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:36.330
that's an obligation for any country.

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:40.140
But when you do that,
you are going to cause

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:41.700
a lot of civilian damages.

00:39:41.700 --> 00:39:43.383
How do you weigh that?

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:46.110
And he said basically, heck, if I know.

00:39:46.110 --> 00:39:46.943
There's a rule war.

00:39:46.943 --> 00:39:50.910
So there's Israel went after commanders,

00:39:50.910 --> 00:39:52.440
but a lot of collateral damage.

00:39:52.440 --> 00:39:53.940
How many, you know?

00:39:53.940 --> 00:39:55.513
So I don't know what to...

00:39:55.513 --> 00:39:56.346
I don't think...

00:39:56.346 --> 00:39:59.940
I think there are some that are, you know,

00:39:59.940 --> 00:40:03.327
clearly don't want Israel
to do anything at all.

00:40:03.327 --> 00:40:07.260
And to me, you know,
that may be an indication

00:40:07.260 --> 00:40:10.110
of something that is problematic.

00:40:10.110 --> 00:40:13.350
Because if the United States
were attacked from Canada,

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:15.510
and then say, "Oh, don't respond,"

00:40:15.510 --> 00:40:17.940
that wouldn't be an appropriate response.

00:40:17.940 --> 00:40:22.940
But to have concerns about human beings

00:40:23.760 --> 00:40:25.290
and thinking about, you know,

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:28.050
other ways forward other than just

00:40:28.050 --> 00:40:29.883
bombing the heck out of the place,

00:40:32.126 --> 00:40:34.560
I think that's reasonable
and not anti-Semitic.

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:36.060
But again, it depends on the context

00:40:36.060 --> 00:40:37.950
and what the motivations are.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:40.800
- Okay, and there are
a few questions about,

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:42.940
you know, trying to understand

00:40:44.010 --> 00:40:47.010
what is motivating some...

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:48.390
And this is a hard question,

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.340
but what's motivating a lot

00:40:50.340 --> 00:40:53.250
of the anti-Israel sentiment right now?

00:40:53.250 --> 00:40:54.140
Is it...

00:40:56.070 --> 00:40:58.290
Do you think that it's, you know,

00:40:58.290 --> 00:41:02.640
unleashing prejudice
that was already there?

00:41:02.640 --> 00:41:07.170
And another way of
putting this is, you know,

00:41:07.170 --> 00:41:09.720
what's motivating, for instance, you know,

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:11.490
I was reading an article yesterday

00:41:11.490 --> 00:41:15.210
about anti-Semitism on
the Chinese internet.

00:41:15.210 --> 00:41:20.210
You know, and so, is that purely sympathy

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:22.110
for Palestinians?

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:25.473
Or do you think that there are
other things going on here?

00:41:26.760 --> 00:41:29.370
- I think there's probably not one simple,

00:41:29.370 --> 00:41:30.330
you know, situation.

00:41:30.330 --> 00:41:31.800
I think there are some that, you know,

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:34.680
glom onto the good and bad.

00:41:34.680 --> 00:41:36.570
And Palestinians are oppressed

00:41:36.570 --> 00:41:38.313
and Israelis are oppressors.

00:41:39.611 --> 00:41:41.577
And they feel some sort
of comfort in that.

00:41:41.577 --> 00:41:46.577
Some of them may be going after Israel

00:41:46.980 --> 00:41:49.410
because it's a Jewish state.

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:53.070
That's certainly, I
suspect some aspect of it.

00:41:53.070 --> 00:41:58.070
But I think some of it is, you know,

00:41:58.110 --> 00:42:00.813
again, seeing the pictures,
having the concerns.

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:07.170
And it's a variety of things
that I think motivate people.

00:42:07.170 --> 00:42:11.190
But as what concerns me is that as long

00:42:11.190 --> 00:42:15.853
as this goes on, that's
going to be a challenge.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:21.423
And, I have friends, non-Jewish,

00:42:22.920 --> 00:42:26.160
who are sympathetic to Israel,

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:29.220
but also at aghast at what's going on,

00:42:29.220 --> 00:42:31.530
and thinking about a
ceasefire, and all that.

00:42:31.530 --> 00:42:36.530
And I dunno at what point do you create,

00:42:37.590 --> 00:42:41.100
you know, some more
antipathy to Israel when,

00:42:41.100 --> 00:42:44.280
if there are 10,000 dead babies, 20,000?

00:42:44.280 --> 00:42:47.670
You know, at what point does that create,

00:42:47.670 --> 00:42:49.260
you know, a tremendous...

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:50.820
Aside from the dead people,

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:52.860
does that create challenges for Israel?

00:42:52.860 --> 00:42:53.880
I don't know, I'm glad I don't have

00:42:53.880 --> 00:42:55.653
to make decisions about what to do.

00:42:56.700 --> 00:43:00.030
- Yeah, so here's a question that I know

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:02.070
is on a lot of people's minds.

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:05.760
How do you think social media has impacted

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:08.400
the landscape of anti-Semitism,

00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:11.073
especially maybe on college campuses?

00:43:12.417 --> 00:43:15.873
- I think it gives an
opportunity for people to,

00:43:17.245 --> 00:43:19.320
you know, put things that are hateful

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:22.740
very quickly out there.

00:43:22.740 --> 00:43:27.740
But to me, there's a whole discussion

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:29.910
about hate and social media,

00:43:29.910 --> 00:43:33.150
and whether things should be regulated

00:43:33.150 --> 00:43:36.423
even before October 7th.

00:43:37.470 --> 00:43:41.730
And to me, it's about the
ideas more than the medium.

00:43:41.730 --> 00:43:43.480
I mean, people worried about hate

00:43:44.430 --> 00:43:46.650
when the printing press was put together.

00:43:46.650 --> 00:43:49.320
And one can make the
case of the most hateful,

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:51.480
you know, thing about
Jews was probably things,

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:53.389
some things in the New Testament, right?

00:43:53.389 --> 00:43:58.389
So television, radio, Father Coughlan,

00:44:01.080 --> 00:44:02.400
early internet sites.

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:03.660
So they're all different ways

00:44:03.660 --> 00:44:06.450
and they all communicate in
ways a little differently.

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:08.430
So I think it's part of the puzzle.

00:44:08.430 --> 00:44:10.710
It allows people to communicate

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.360
and build sense of community.

00:44:12.360 --> 00:44:14.360
But I think it also gives an opportunity

00:44:15.330 --> 00:44:16.860
that we haven't fully tapped yet.

00:44:16.860 --> 00:44:19.200
It's one of the things in the center

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:20.370
that we're looking at is,

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:21.600
how do we use social media

00:44:21.600 --> 00:44:23.370
more effectively against hate?

00:44:23.370 --> 00:44:28.350
I mean, to me, I'm sort of aghast that,

00:44:28.350 --> 00:44:30.270
you know, we see hate crimes,

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:34.237
and there's no real easy
mechanism for people to use,

00:44:34.237 --> 00:44:39.090
you know, social media to
know what their resources are,

00:44:39.090 --> 00:44:41.160
how to report a hate
crime, all those things.

00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:43.620
So there are ways of
building up our capacity

00:44:43.620 --> 00:44:46.859
to fight hate a lot
better using social media.

00:44:46.859 --> 00:44:48.600
It's a double-edged sword.

00:44:48.600 --> 00:44:51.960
And to give you one final example of that,

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:55.050
just so that people
understand the context.

00:44:55.050 --> 00:45:00.050
I mean, back at the time of
the Oklahoma City bombing,

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:02.910
it was the early internet days.

00:45:02.910 --> 00:45:04.560
And there were a bunch of us watching

00:45:04.560 --> 00:45:07.257
the militia movement
with its anti-Semitism

00:45:07.257 --> 00:45:10.830
and its hatred of federal officials

00:45:10.830 --> 00:45:13.950
really start taking shape
from one group to two groups,

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:15.900
10 groups, and 20 groups
in a couple of months

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:19.830
all around the country,
more than that even.

00:45:19.830 --> 00:45:22.170
And so, a bunch of us got together

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:25.740
and were watching what they were posting

00:45:25.740 --> 00:45:27.060
on these early bulletin boards.

00:45:27.060 --> 00:45:31.980
So it gave the haters an
opportunity to feel community,

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:35.040
to spread rumors, to organize in a way

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:36.990
that they hadn't had before.

00:45:36.990 --> 00:45:39.270
But it gave us an opportunity to also get

00:45:39.270 --> 00:45:40.680
a better sense of what they were doing,

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:42.360
which is why a bunch of us put a warning

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:46.260
of watch for April 19th,
the anniversary of Waco.

00:45:46.260 --> 00:45:47.940
Something's likely to happen.

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:50.522
So most of these things
are double-edged swords.

00:45:50.522 --> 00:45:52.080
- Yeah, that's a great point.

00:45:52.080 --> 00:45:55.800
Yeah, how can we use social
media to solve the problem,

00:45:55.800 --> 00:45:58.260
rather than just let it keep causing it?

00:45:58.260 --> 00:46:01.740
So a question about statements.

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:04.890
You know, I think that after October 7th,

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:09.890
immediately there seemed to
be this war over statements.

00:46:10.020 --> 00:46:13.050
And especially, I think
on university campuses,

00:46:13.050 --> 00:46:15.720
you know, were university Presidents

00:46:15.720 --> 00:46:17.490
quick to put out a statement.

00:46:17.490 --> 00:46:20.160
Were the statements
sufficiently pro-Israel,

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.830
or were they too pro-Israel?

00:46:22.830 --> 00:46:27.660
How do you explain just the passion

00:46:27.660 --> 00:46:31.200
that the statement question aroused?

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:35.309
And do you think statements
are a good thing?

00:46:35.309 --> 00:46:37.953
What's your statement on statements?

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:41.310
- Well, I think people
sort of see it as a symbol.

00:46:41.310 --> 00:46:44.640
And they try almost
with Talmudic intensity,

00:46:44.640 --> 00:46:46.800
try to dissect it and doesn't...

00:46:46.800 --> 00:46:48.300
You know, is it sufficient?

00:46:48.300 --> 00:46:49.133
Is it insufficient?

00:46:49.133 --> 00:46:51.750
Does it cover what I care about?

00:46:51.750 --> 00:46:54.540
Or does it go to the other side?

00:46:54.540 --> 00:46:56.730
Is it all those things?

00:46:56.730 --> 00:47:01.590
This a University of Chicago-based report

00:47:01.590 --> 00:47:04.140
that came out in the '60s
called "The Calvin Report"

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:07.950
that basically said universities really

00:47:07.950 --> 00:47:12.780
ought to avoid stepping into
making political statements.

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:15.960
And I think, basically, that's right.

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:19.530
I mean, I did two
statements when I was at HJC

00:47:19.530 --> 00:47:23.310
that got over 400 college
Presidents to sign.

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:25.470
One was when we saw attacks

00:47:25.470 --> 00:47:27.690
on the Hillel Building at Berkeley,

00:47:27.690 --> 00:47:31.800
and Concordia was a riot in 2002

00:47:31.800 --> 00:47:34.080
with Jehuda Reinharz,
who's in at Brandeis,

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:35.970
and the late Jim Friedman from Dartmouth,

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.910
and a bunch of others who
put together a statement

00:47:38.910 --> 00:47:41.430
that talked about the college President's

00:47:41.430 --> 00:47:43.500
responsibility to the campus,

00:47:43.500 --> 00:47:47.370
to not allow harassment,
not allow intimidation,

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:49.800
to keep it open to ideas, and so forth.

00:47:49.800 --> 00:47:52.530
And then in 2007, I
worked with Lee Bollinger

00:47:52.530 --> 00:47:54.450
and about 400 other college Presidents,

00:47:54.450 --> 00:47:56.400
including Harold Shapiro of Princeton,

00:47:56.400 --> 00:48:01.320
it was when the University
and College Union in the UK

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:02.675
put out a statement advancing

00:48:02.675 --> 00:48:05.160
an academic book out of Israel.

00:48:05.160 --> 00:48:07.560
And so, we got about 400 some odds

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:10.590
to sign onto Lee's statement, which was,

00:48:10.590 --> 00:48:13.230
if that union was intent on dividing

00:48:13.230 --> 00:48:16.353
the academic world into
Israelis, who should be shunned,

00:48:17.190 --> 00:48:20.910
and everybody else, count us as Israelis.

00:48:20.910 --> 00:48:22.530
This is the same time
the ADL was floating,

00:48:22.530 --> 00:48:26.280
gee, maybe we should boycott
British academic institutions,

00:48:26.280 --> 00:48:30.000
which in my view was
ridiculous to change the debate

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:31.650
from should we have academic boycotts

00:48:31.650 --> 00:48:32.910
to who should be boycott?

00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:35.460
But the principle of
both of those statements

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:38.010
was looking at the campus
and academic freedom

00:48:38.010 --> 00:48:38.843
and what the campus should be.

00:48:38.843 --> 00:48:42.090
And "The Calvin Report"
says that that's fine.

00:48:42.090 --> 00:48:45.330
So what I was advising
when this was happening is,

00:48:45.330 --> 00:48:49.050
yeah, I think it's
important for Presidents

00:48:49.050 --> 00:48:52.080
not to be seen as silent
and ignoring this.

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:54.240
I think it's perfectly appropriate to put

00:48:54.240 --> 00:48:57.423
out something that says we
know our students are hurting.

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:00.990
I think, and to what are the resources?

00:49:00.990 --> 00:49:03.210
I think it's important to
talk about what the campus

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:06.600
can do at this time,
this educational mission,

00:49:06.600 --> 00:49:09.420
and how we should be treating each other.

00:49:09.420 --> 00:49:13.050
But to make a statement
about, even though personally,

00:49:13.050 --> 00:49:15.480
you know, I would've deplored, obviously,

00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:16.683
what Hamas was doing,

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:19.737
as a President, I probably would not have.

00:49:19.737 --> 00:49:22.560
And I think Maud Mandel at Williams

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:24.750
put out a really smart statement.

00:49:24.750 --> 00:49:26.340
It was a statement about why she wasn't

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:27.930
putting out a statement.

00:49:27.930 --> 00:49:31.200
But it was very clear
that she was focusing

00:49:31.200 --> 00:49:34.410
on what the campus
should do as opposed to,

00:49:34.410 --> 00:49:36.780
you know, don't expect
the President's Office

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:38.370
to be the State Department.

00:49:38.370 --> 00:49:41.217
- Right, but I understand that.

00:49:41.217 --> 00:49:44.040
And I am sort of against statements also.

00:49:44.040 --> 00:49:45.720
I wish that they had never gone

00:49:45.720 --> 00:49:46.830
down that road to begin with.

00:49:46.830 --> 00:49:49.200
But I can also understand
the other point of view,

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:52.320
which is once universities
start putting out statements

00:49:52.320 --> 00:49:57.090
when some groups are hurt,
why are Jews left out?

00:49:57.090 --> 00:49:59.340
You know, why doesn't the massacre of Jews

00:49:59.340 --> 00:50:00.960
merit a statement, or why?

00:50:00.960 --> 00:50:05.730
You know, so I do
understand that side also.

00:50:05.730 --> 00:50:08.160
- Right, but I understand that too.

00:50:08.160 --> 00:50:09.660
And that's why I think it's probably

00:50:09.660 --> 00:50:12.660
a better policy not to put it out.

00:50:12.660 --> 00:50:17.130
But I don't remember anybody,

00:50:17.130 --> 00:50:19.410
you know, getting up on campus and...

00:50:19.410 --> 00:50:20.460
There may have been some students,

00:50:20.460 --> 00:50:23.070
but I don't remember
people organizing saying,

00:50:23.070 --> 00:50:25.743
you know, that George Floyd got it coming.

00:50:26.940 --> 00:50:30.720
Or back in those apartheid
South Africa days,

00:50:30.720 --> 00:50:33.090
somebody saying apartheid is a good thing,

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:33.923
- Right.

00:50:33.923 --> 00:50:38.280
- On this issue, you have
a lot of divisiveness

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:39.450
on the campus.

00:50:39.450 --> 00:50:43.590
And so, I don't see campuses

00:50:43.590 --> 00:50:45.900
putting out positions on abortion.

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:49.290
I don't see campuses saying, you know,

00:50:49.290 --> 00:50:52.260
leave aside the 501(c)(3) problems.

00:50:52.260 --> 00:50:53.430
You know, you should be opposing Trump.

00:50:53.430 --> 00:50:55.200
- Right, but I'm just
gonna push back on that

00:50:55.200 --> 00:50:56.340
because I think you could say,

00:50:56.340 --> 00:51:01.200
well, but isn't the
massacre of innocent people

00:51:01.200 --> 00:51:04.530
in Israel like an inherent moral,

00:51:04.530 --> 00:51:07.860
you know, catastrophe that needs

00:51:07.860 --> 00:51:10.290
to be denounced at that time, isn't it?

00:51:10.290 --> 00:51:13.680
And isn't it the responsibility
of the university

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:16.683
to take a clear moral stance
on something like that?

00:51:18.600 --> 00:51:22.533
- The answer is I, as
an individual, would.

00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:27.930
But as a university President,
I'd be very hesitant to.

00:51:27.930 --> 00:51:29.287
I mean, they haven't put
out statements about,

00:51:29.287 --> 00:51:31.650
you know, the earthquake in Turkey.

00:51:31.650 --> 00:51:34.410
They haven't put out statements
about a lot of other things.

00:51:34.410 --> 00:51:35.880
I don't remember about the Uyghurs.

00:51:35.880 --> 00:51:37.382
You know, I mean, you could talk this...

00:51:37.382 --> 00:51:42.382
This was an atrocity that
was just unfathomable.

00:51:45.600 --> 00:51:49.110
And I think there are
opportunities on a campus

00:51:49.110 --> 00:51:50.490
to educate about this.

00:51:50.490 --> 00:51:54.797
And I am concerned about students
who are so in that tunnel,

00:51:56.130 --> 00:51:59.160
that that can't even to tick a box,

00:51:59.160 --> 00:52:02.220
say, "You know, killing babies
is not such a good thing.

00:52:02.220 --> 00:52:04.680
But we still were supporting
for Palestinian rights."

00:52:04.680 --> 00:52:05.513
- Right.

00:52:05.513 --> 00:52:07.050
- All we're saying...

00:52:07.050 --> 00:52:08.310
You know, I have have a colleague

00:52:08.310 --> 00:52:10.530
who teaches at Rutgers who was telling me

00:52:10.530 --> 00:52:12.570
that one of the pro-Hamas students

00:52:12.570 --> 00:52:15.510
was a student who thought
Hamas was a person.

00:52:15.510 --> 00:52:17.250
There's a lot of ignorance out there,

00:52:17.250 --> 00:52:19.407
and not knowing about what the Hamas is,

00:52:19.407 --> 00:52:22.530
and its original charter, and
how it treats gays and women.

00:52:22.530 --> 00:52:25.230
And so, there's a lot of education there,

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:30.230
but there is some, again, it's to me,

00:52:32.280 --> 00:52:35.070
I'm more interested in
what a college President

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:37.050
does to use the educational-

00:52:37.050 --> 00:52:39.890
- Right, I was just say...

00:52:41.086 --> 00:52:46.086
I agree that statements are
inherently reductionist.

00:52:46.110 --> 00:52:48.840
You know, and it's kind
of contrary to the mission

00:52:48.840 --> 00:52:50.880
of what the university is supposed to do,

00:52:50.880 --> 00:52:54.330
which is to give deep
context and to teach.

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:55.200
So this leads to...

00:52:55.200 --> 00:52:56.550
'Cause we're sort of running outta time,

00:52:56.550 --> 00:52:59.100
it leads to some other questions.

00:52:59.100 --> 00:53:03.870
People pushing back on
the call precisely for,

00:53:03.870 --> 00:53:06.120
you know, the response to anti-Semitism

00:53:06.120 --> 00:53:07.713
being more education.

00:53:09.414 --> 00:53:14.414
How do you do that when
people seem so dug in on this

00:53:14.430 --> 00:53:17.223
issue that they just don't wanna learn,

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:21.570
or they're just so
completely ignorant that

00:53:21.570 --> 00:53:24.600
they're just not willing to learn

00:53:24.600 --> 00:53:29.600
and are taking these knee-jerk
positions on either side.

00:53:30.270 --> 00:53:33.690
How then does education become the answer

00:53:33.690 --> 00:53:38.520
in a situation like that
that's so resistant to it?

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:40.470
- Yeah, it's a great question.

00:53:40.470 --> 00:53:45.470
And I think part of, again,
what a university needs to do

00:53:45.480 --> 00:53:48.480
is broader than just
looking at this question.

00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:52.080
It's the culture of
being disturbed by ideas

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:54.270
of finding new classes.

00:53:54.270 --> 00:53:56.700
Again, new classes on anti-Semitism,

00:53:56.700 --> 00:54:00.090
new classes on hatred, and you know,

00:54:00.090 --> 00:54:03.393
the why people hate and why
we get into these boxes,

00:54:04.770 --> 00:54:08.160
more programs that bring in people

00:54:08.160 --> 00:54:10.710
with different points of view to model

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:15.090
how you can disagree on
strongly divergent views,

00:54:15.090 --> 00:54:17.670
classes on how to do that.

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:21.033
Those things I think over
time will set the tone.

00:54:22.140 --> 00:54:24.510
And it comes from the
university leadership,

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:26.370
it comes from the faculty.

00:54:26.370 --> 00:54:29.218
And the more we can
empower those voices...

00:54:29.218 --> 00:54:31.290
Is it gonna be like a silver bullet?

00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:34.950
No, but I think ultimately
that's the best tool

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:36.840
that a campus has.

00:54:36.840 --> 00:54:38.880
And when it goes down
the other road of saying,

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:41.820
we find this speech so deplorable

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:42.653
that we're gonna censor it,

00:54:42.653 --> 00:54:44.490
it just feeds into the binary,

00:54:44.490 --> 00:54:47.400
which is the problem in the first place.

00:54:47.400 --> 00:54:51.180
There's also an opportunity here that,

00:54:51.180 --> 00:54:54.060
you know, campuses have a turnover

00:54:54.060 --> 00:54:56.730
at least a quarter of
their students every year.

00:54:56.730 --> 00:55:01.350
So are you gonna, with programs
now, change a lot of minds?

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.390
Probably not, although, I think some were

00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:06.390
changing the program that I participated,

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:08.640
I'm sure programs in other places.

00:55:08.640 --> 00:55:12.810
But you have next year and the year after.

00:55:12.810 --> 00:55:15.330
And I think, it's more of an expectation

00:55:15.330 --> 00:55:18.690
when students come in of,
how do you treat each other?

00:55:18.690 --> 00:55:20.776
How do you understand?

00:55:20.776 --> 00:55:23.610
Those things will help set
that up a little bit better.

00:55:23.610 --> 00:55:27.150
- And okay, that leads
into another question

00:55:27.150 --> 00:55:28.050
that someone had, which is,

00:55:28.050 --> 00:55:31.980
how should university administrations

00:55:31.980 --> 00:55:36.980
manage these protests,
anti-Israel protests,

00:55:39.270 --> 00:55:41.070
to make sure that they don't become

00:55:41.070 --> 00:55:43.803
opportunities for anti-Semitic expression?

00:55:46.230 --> 00:55:51.230
- I think anti-Semitic
expressions are awful,

00:55:52.440 --> 00:55:54.000
but they're 1st Amendment protected.

00:55:54.000 --> 00:55:54.833
So you're not gonna...

00:55:54.833 --> 00:55:57.921
You're gonna punish a
student who's gonna say...

00:55:57.921 --> 00:56:01.800
You know, like in Cornell,
make true threats.

00:56:01.800 --> 00:56:03.420
Be very clear on that.

00:56:03.420 --> 00:56:08.420
But you also want to
encourage the student body

00:56:10.500 --> 00:56:12.510
to realize that there are things

00:56:12.510 --> 00:56:15.510
that are being said
that are really hurtful

00:56:15.510 --> 00:56:17.490
or perceived as hurtful by people,

00:56:17.490 --> 00:56:20.070
and to understand the
more of the context of it.

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:21.930
So if they're gonna take a position

00:56:21.930 --> 00:56:24.393
that I, as a Zionist, feel,

00:56:26.670 --> 00:56:28.080
accustom me to the quick.

00:56:28.080 --> 00:56:33.030
They at least ought to
know how to deal with that.

00:56:33.030 --> 00:56:34.440
And on the flip side, again,

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:37.330
is to have a perception that

00:56:38.820 --> 00:56:42.030
when you hear something,
you're not fragile.

00:56:42.030 --> 00:56:44.250
You're gonna figure
out how you're gonna...

00:56:44.250 --> 00:56:46.920
I remember at Bard years ago,

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:48.180
I walked onto the campus

00:56:48.180 --> 00:56:51.030
and there was a big free Palestine sign.

00:56:51.030 --> 00:56:52.230
I had not seen that before.

00:56:52.230 --> 00:56:54.720
And it sort of made me very uncomfortable.

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:55.917
And then, I thought about it.

00:56:55.917 --> 00:56:58.710
And I thought, that's
somebody has an opinion

00:56:58.710 --> 00:57:00.570
I strongly disagree with.

00:57:00.570 --> 00:57:03.810
I understand it makes
me deeply uncomfortable.

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:07.050
But I would be more uncomfortable

00:57:07.050 --> 00:57:09.060
if the administration said

00:57:09.060 --> 00:57:10.800
they can't express themselves.

00:57:10.800 --> 00:57:12.566
And again, finding opportunities.

00:57:12.566 --> 00:57:13.860
There are ways.

00:57:13.860 --> 00:57:16.320
I mean, there are small
groups to bring together,

00:57:16.320 --> 00:57:18.060
which I've done of people that are

00:57:18.060 --> 00:57:19.830
strongly opposed on this issue,

00:57:19.830 --> 00:57:21.900
but want to have the conversation,

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:24.990
and with text study and so forth.

00:57:24.990 --> 00:57:27.150
Does it necessarily change minds?

00:57:27.150 --> 00:57:29.430
No, but it's a possibility.

00:57:29.430 --> 00:57:31.020
I'll give you one other
example and this is,

00:57:31.020 --> 00:57:34.020
I have a blueprint for this
at the end of the book.

00:57:34.020 --> 00:57:35.700
It was a class I love that a woman

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:37.277
named Natasha Gill came up with.

00:57:37.277 --> 00:57:40.830
It was a simulation course.

00:57:40.830 --> 00:57:44.880
So half the course was students
basically being actors.

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:47.580
And the course was looking

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:50.550
at the Peel Commission of the '30s.

00:57:50.550 --> 00:57:53.520
So for those who don't recall, the Brits,

00:57:53.520 --> 00:57:55.710
when they had the mandates
sent Lord Peel to figure out,

00:57:55.710 --> 00:57:57.240
what the heck are we gonna do with this?

00:57:57.240 --> 00:57:59.340
And they talked to
everybody, Arabs and Jews,

00:57:59.340 --> 00:58:04.340
and came up with a report of what to do.

00:58:04.350 --> 00:58:08.227
So this is before the
Holocaust, before '48,

00:58:09.346 --> 00:58:11.970
before '67, before contemporary times,

00:58:11.970 --> 00:58:13.860
so the guts of the conflict.

00:58:13.860 --> 00:58:15.630
And it was well structured.

00:58:15.630 --> 00:58:18.760
She gave students roles opposite

00:58:20.160 --> 00:58:21.420
that would be an easy one for her.

00:58:21.420 --> 00:58:24.295
So she gave a Pakistani Muslim,

00:58:24.295 --> 00:58:26.520
had to be eight weeks faithfully

00:58:26.520 --> 00:58:28.740
representing David Ben-Gurion.

00:58:28.740 --> 00:58:31.380
And when I talked to the student about it,

00:58:31.380 --> 00:58:36.047
she said, "You know, as
strange as that was for me,

00:58:36.047 --> 00:58:38.700
it was probably stranger
for my Israeli classmate,

00:58:38.700 --> 00:58:40.230
who had to be the mufti.

00:58:40.230 --> 00:58:42.210
And as much as a pain in the butt he was,

00:58:42.210 --> 00:58:46.590
the Jabotinsky figure on my
side was worse to deal with,

00:58:46.590 --> 00:58:49.200
so like a little bit of reality.

00:58:49.200 --> 00:58:52.470
And what I'd loved about
it is Natasha told me that,

00:58:52.470 --> 00:58:54.097
you know, students would come in and say,

00:58:54.097 --> 00:58:56.160
"Gee, we can figure out how this conflict

00:58:56.160 --> 00:58:59.760
should have been resolved 80 years ago."

00:58:59.760 --> 00:59:00.817
And they come out of it saying,

00:59:00.817 --> 00:59:02.310
"Now, we understand why it hasn't been

00:59:02.310 --> 00:59:03.600
resolved in 80 years."

00:59:03.600 --> 00:59:04.590
That's education.

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:07.080
And the more things we can
do like that, the better.

